    <h2>The Myth: All Lawn Guys Are the Same</h2>

    <p>The belief underneath most landscaping decisions in Hartselle is that lawn care is a commodity — every crew mows, so pick the cheapest one. It sounds reasonable and it is why the market is a race to the bottom on price. The myth holds right up until you notice that two houses in the same subdivision, paying similar money, have completely different lawns: one thick and green, one thin and full of weeds no matter how often it gets cut. Same climate, same soil, same rain. The difference is not the mower. It is whether anyone is actually managing the lawn or just shortening it.</p>

    <h2>The Reality: The Work Is in the Soil</h2>

    <p>A healthy Hartselle lawn is a soil-and-timing problem before it is a mowing problem. The county's red clay compacts hard and drains slowly, which strangles roots, so core aeration is not optional here the way it is in loose soil. Grass type matters too — this is transition-zone country where warm-season lawns like bermuda and zoysia and cool-season fescue all get planted, and each wants a different cut height and a different feeding schedule. Soil pH for fescue wants to sit around 5.5 to 6.5, and Alabama clay often runs off that mark, which no amount of mowing corrects. A landscaper who knows this reads your yard; a mowing crew just visits it.</p>

    <h2>What the Good Ones Do That the Cheap Ones Don't</h2>

    <p>The real operators do the invisible work. They set mowing height to the grass type and follow the one-third rule — never removing more than a third of the blade in a cut, because scalping stresses the lawn and invites weeds. They avoid mowing wet clay, which compacts it and ruts it. They aerate, they test and amend soil instead of guessing, and they grade beds and lawns to move water away from the house. On the newer subdivision lots where builders scraped the topsoil and left bare red clay, and on the designed landscapes out in higher-end areas like Bethel Ridge, that grading-and-soil competence is the entire difference between a yard that thrives and one that fights you. Drainage in particular ties landscaping to the hardscape trades — a regrade often pairs with new <a href="/locations/hartselle/concrete/">concrete</a> walkways or edging, and a fresh bed line frequently goes in the same season as a new <a href="/locations/hartselle/fencing/">fence</a>.</p>

    <h2>Red Flags When You're Hiring</h2>

    <p>A few tells separate a mowing service quoting a landscaping job from someone who can actually deliver one. They quote your whole yard sight-unseen off the square footage without walking the drainage. They have no answer when you ask what grass type you have or what your soil needs. They mow every lawn to the same height regardless of species. And they go silent or vague when you raise the standing water by the foundation — because grading is real work they are not set up to do. The honest admission cuts both ways: if all you want is a tidy weekly cut, a mow-and-blow crew at $35 a visit is the right, sensible buy, and you should not pay design prices for it. Just do not expect a thriving, problem-solved landscape from a mowing budget — that is a different service.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Landscaping Costs in Hartselle</h2>

    <p>Real ranges:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Basic mowing</strong> — about $35 to $50 per visit for a typical lot.</li>
      <li><strong>Full-service maintenance</strong> — roughly $150 to $300 a month with edging, cleanup, fertilization, and bed care.</li>
      <li><strong>Drainage work (French drain, regrade)</strong> — about $1,200 to $3,500 depending on length and depth.</li>
      <li><strong>Design and installation</strong> — beds, plantings, and hardscape, quoted per project.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The number that matters is the jump from mowing to maintenance: it is not a markup on the same service, it is a different service that keeps the lawn alive instead of just short.</p>

    <h2>The Smart Play</h2>

    <p>Decide what you are actually buying before you shop. If it is a clean weekly cut, hire on price and reliability and be happy. If it is a lawn and landscape that looks like the best house on the street, hire the person who talks about soil and drainage before they talk about mowing, and treat the higher number as the price of results rather than an upsell. Spring is the moment to start — aeration, soil correction, and the season's plan all land best before summer, and a yard set up right in April is the one that holds through August.</p>

    <h2>Questions Hartselle Homeowners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>Why is my lawn thin if I pay for weekly mowing?</strong></p>
    <p>Mowing isn't lawn care. Compacted red clay, wrong cut height, and untended soil are usually the cause. Aeration and soil work fix what mowing can't.</p>

    <p><strong>How do I stop water pooling by the foundation?</strong></p>
    <p>Grading and drainage — regrade to fall away from the house, add a French drain if needed. It's design work, not a mowing task.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>Mowing $35 to $50 a visit; full maintenance $150 to $300 a month; drainage $1,200 to $3,500.</p>

